        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 98 seconds] Tutor is in the room alone, waiting. The tutor navigates between the \"Intro\", \"Warm Up\", and \"Whiteboard 7\" tabs. The \"Intro\" tab shows two \"Would you rather\" questions. The \"Warm Up\" tab shows various math problems. The \"Whiteboard 7\" tab is blank.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 11 seconds] A notification \"Jaylen has joined\" appears on the screen.\nThe screen shows the \"Intro\" tab with the following text:\nIntro\n-Would you rather take a super hard class with your favorite teacher or an easy class with your least favorite teacher?\n-Would you rather have a rewind button or a pause button for your life?\nTutor clicks on the \"Warm Up\" tab.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen changes to a document titled \"Negatives\". A notification appears that says \"Jaylen has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears that says \"Mr. Davis has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 67 seconds]\nTurn 1. STUDENT: Hi Mr. Davis.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: Hey, Jaylen.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: Good morning.\nTurn 4. STUDENT: Good morning.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: All right. Um, we can just start here with these two questions. Uh, would you rather take a super hard class with your favorite teacher or an easy class with your least favorite teacher?\nTurn 6. STUDENT: A super I would rather take a super hard class with my favorite teacher.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Me too. Yeah. It makes such a difference with that. Definitely better with good teachers.\nTurn 8. TUTOR: Um, would you rather have a rewind button or a pause button for your life?\nTurn 9. STUDENT: And what's a rewind?\nTurn 10. TUTOR: Like, you could rewind time. Like, go back, like, I don't know, an hour.\nTurn 11. STUDENT: Probably pause.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Okay. Okay. Wait, why?\nTurn 13. STUDENT: Then like, if if I say pause, does it mean like no one can move and I can move?\nTurn 14. TUTOR: Yeah.\nTurn 15. STUDENT: Yeah, I'm doing pause.\nTurn 16. STUDENT: Now I know I could do all these things.\nTurn 17. TUTOR: That's I think I would also choose the same thing. Just to take a break from life for a second and just be like, yeah.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: Okay, good. Cool. Perfect. Um, we can just do the warm up here now.\nTurn 19. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'What number is missing from the diagram?' (start)] The screen shows a problem with a sequence of numbers in boxes: 14, 21, 28, 35, and an empty box. Below are four multiple-choice options: A) 36, B) 41, C) 42, D) 49.\nTurn 19. STUDENT: Um, get some Are we doing math?\nTurn 20. TUTOR: Yes, math today. No, no passage reading.\nTurn 21. TUTOR: Uh, what number is missing from this diagram here?\nTurn 22. STUDENT: Okay, let's see. Uh, um, where is my paper?\nTurn 23. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 18 seconds]\nTurn 23. STUDENT: I'll be right back.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: Okay, yeah. Take your time.\nTurn 25. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 63 seconds] The student's camera feed goes black. The tutor waits and briefly scrolls to other problems in the \"Warm Up\" tab.\nOn the whiteboard, a problem is displayed: \"What number is missing from the diagram?\". Below this, a sequence of numbers is shown in boxes: 14, 21, 28, 35, and an empty box. Below the sequence are four multiple-choice options labeled A, B, C, and D with corresponding numbers 36, 41, 42, and 49.\nTurn 25. STUDENT: Okay, I'm back.\nTurn 26. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 23 seconds] Tutor's cursor moves to option D.\nTurn 27. STUDENT: Uh.\nTurn 28. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 9 seconds]\nTurn 28. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student/tutor - pointing] Tutor's cursor points to the number 14. Student's cursor points to option C, then to option D.\nTutor's cursor moves to the empty box in the number sequence.\nStudent draws a green checkmark next to the number 14.\nTutor draws an arrow from 28 to 35 and writes \"+7\". Tutor then circles option C, \"42\".\nTurn 28. TUTOR: Are you uh, you're adding?\nTurn 29. STUDENT: C.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: C, forty-two. And what what rule did you figure it out?\nTurn 31. STUDENT: First I did twenty-one minus fourteen. And then that equaled seven and then I did thirty-five minus seven. I mean thirty-five plus seven.\nTurn 32. TUTOR: Right, exactly. Yeah, because the rule is just adding seven. Yeah. Perfect. Okay. That's correct.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p2 - '46 is equivalent to' (start)] The screen scrolls down to a new problem: \"46 is equivalent to\". The options are A) 6 tens and 4 ones, B) 6 ones and 4 tens, C) 4 tens and 60 ones, D) 40 tens and 6 ones.\nStudent's cursor moves over option B.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: All right. Uh, I scrolled down a little bit, so you may have to scroll on your screen unless you saw this one, but it says forty-six.\nTurn 34. STUDENT: Yeah, I can see it already.\nTurn 35. TUTOR: Oh, so you see this when I scroll, right? Yeah. Okay, cool.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: Uh, forty-six is equivalent to...\nTurn 37. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 10 seconds] Tutor writes \"60\" on the whiteboard and then draws several hash marks on the right side.\nTutor points to the '4' in the number 46.\nTutor circles option C, \"4 tens and 60 ones\".\nTutor draws a circle around option B.\nTurn 37. STUDENT: C.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
